{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"os-module","title":"Files & Folders with os","group":"Working with Files","order":2,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The <code>os</code> module talks to the operating system: where you are, what's in a folder, and creating, renaming or removing directories — the plumbing behind organising datasets.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-map-pin\"></i>Where am I?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os\nstart = os.getcwd()              # current working directory\nos.mkdir(\"project\")              # create one folder\nos.chdir(\"project\")              # move into it\nprint(os.path.basename(os.getcwd()))   # project\nos.chdir(\"..\")                   # back to the parent\nprint(os.getcwd() == start)      # True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>os.getcwd()</code></td><td>Current working directory</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.chdir(path)</code></td><td>Change directory (<code>..</code> = parent)</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.listdir(path='.')</code></td><td>Names of files and folders in a directory</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.mkdir(path)</code></td><td>Create <strong>one</strong> folder (parent must exist)</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.makedirs(path, exist_ok=True)</code></td><td>Create a whole chain of folders</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.rmdir(path)</code></td><td>Remove an <strong>empty</strong> folder</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.rename(old, new)</code></td><td>Rename or move</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.remove(path)</code></td><td>Delete a file</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.path.join(a, b)</code></td><td>Build a path with the right separator</td></tr><tr><td><code>os.path.exists(p)</code> / <code>isdir</code> / <code>isfile</code></td><td>Check what's there</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-folder\"></i>Nested folders</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os\nos.makedirs(\"data/raw/2026\", exist_ok=True)     # creates data, data/raw and data/raw/2026\nos.rmdir(\"data/raw/2026\")                       # only works because it's empty\nos.rename(\"data\", \"dataset\")\nprint(sorted(os.listdir(\"dataset\")))            # ['raw']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>os.rmdir</code> refuses non-empty folders. To delete a whole tree use <code>shutil.rmtree(path)</code> — carefully: there's no recycle bin.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Creating folders in bulk</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os\nmaster = \"KT\"\nn, m = 5, 5\nfor i in range(1, n + 1):\n    for j in range(1, m + 1):\n        os.makedirs(os.path.join(master, f\"{master} {i}\", f\"{master} {i}.{j}\"), exist_ok=True)\n\ntotal = sum(len(dirs) for _, dirs, _ in os.walk(master)) + 1   # + the master folder itself\nprint(total)                                                    # 31</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>That's the notebook's <code>n*m + n + 1</code> = 5×5 + 5 + 1 folders. <code>os.walk(top)</code> yields <code>(folder, subfolders, files)</code> for every folder under <code>top</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-files\"></i>Reading many text files</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import os\nos.makedirs(\"Files\", exist_ok=True)\nfor name, text in [(\"a.txt\", \"first\"), (\"b.txt\", \"second\"), (\".DS_Store\", \"junk\")]:\n    with open(os.path.join(\"Files\", name), \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(text)\n\ncontents = []\nfor name in sorted(os.listdir(\"Files\")):\n    if name.startswith(\".\"):                    # skip hidden files like .DS_Store\n        continue\n    with open(os.path.join(\"Files\", name)) as f:\n        contents.append(f.read())\nprint(contents)                                 # ['first', 'second']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Tip</div><p>Modern alternative: <code>pathlib</code> — <code>[p.read_text() for p in Path(\"Files\").glob(\"*.txt\")]</code> does the same in one line.</p></div></section>"}